    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T17:30:28.455Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/) + HPC schedulers for accelerator-heavy AI.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[Slurm vs Kubernetes for GPUs]\n  N0 --> N1[TPU pods]\n  N1 --> N2[Operators and RBAC]\n  N2 --> N3[Ingress/egress controls]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Infra Q A GPU/TPU clusters Slurm Kuberne))\n    GPU Clusters\n    TPU Pods\n    HPC Systems\n    Slurm\n    Kubernetes Operators\n    Kubernetes RBAC\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Slurm vs Kubernetes for GPUs?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSlurm: HPC batch training on shared clusters. Kubernetes: service-oriented training/serving with operators (GPU device plugins, JobSets). Enterprises often run both.\n\n### Q2. TPU pods?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nGoogle’s accelerator slices for large-scale training — great when the stack targets JAX/TF on GCP; plan data locality and checkpointing.\n\n### Q3. Operators and RBAC?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOperators encode operational knowledge (deploy Triton, Spark). RBAC least-privileges humans/service accounts. Replace legacy PSPs with Pod Security Admission.\n\n### Q4. Ingress/egress controls?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nIngress for north-south API exposure; egress policies to lock which model APIs/data stores workloads can reach — critical for secret and cost control.\n\n## Official references\n- [Kubernetes docs](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/) · [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/)\n",
