    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T17:30:28.439Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nGovernance-friendly data architecture for ML/GenAI (technical focus).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[Data mesh vs fabric]\n  N0 --> N1[Why lineage and catalogs]\n  N1 --> N2[Parquet vs ORC vs Avro]\n  N2 --> N3[AI-specific metadata]\n  N3 --> N4[Key takeaway]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Data Q A Mesh fabric lineage catalogs Av))\n    Data Mesh\n    Data Fabric\n    Metadata Management\n    Data Lineage\n    Data Catalogs\n    Data Lakes\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Data mesh vs fabric?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nMesh: domain-owned data products with federated governance. Fabric: integrated platform layer stitching sources. Many orgs blend both.\n\n### Q2. Why lineage and catalogs?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nKnow where training/RAG data came from, who owns it, and what breaks if a column changes. Catalogs enable discovery; lineage enables impact analysis.\n\n### Q3. Parquet vs ORC vs Avro?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nParquet/ORC: columnar analytics. Avro: row-oriented, great for evolving event schemas. Delta/Iceberg add ACID on lakes.\n\n### Q4. AI-specific metadata?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nChunking policy, embedding model version, PII tags, retention, and license — store beside the corpus.\n\n## Key takeaway\nWithout lineage/catalogs, RAG and training datasets become un-debuggable.\n",
