    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T17:30:28.316Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Feast](https://docs.feast.dev/) and graph-augmented retrieval/features.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[What problem does Feast solv]\n  N0 --> N1[Online vs offline store]\n  N1 --> N2[Where Neo4j/graph AI helps]\n  N2 --> N3[Quality checks]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Tools Q A Feast feature store and Neo4j ))\n    Feast\n    Feature Store\n    Neo4j Graph AI\n    Graph Databases\n    Online Features\n    Offline Features\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What problem does Feast solve?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nConsistent offline (training) vs online (serving) features, point-in-time joins, and feature discovery — reducing training/serving skew.\n\n### Q2. Online vs offline store?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOffline: historical batches (Parquet/warehouse). Online: low-latency KV for inference. Materialization jobs keep them aligned.\n\n### Q3. Where Neo4j/graph AI helps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRelationships (entities, citations, org charts) that vectors alone miss. Use graph queries + embeddings for hybrid RAG.\n\n### Q4. Quality checks?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nMonitor null rates, drift, freshness SLAs, and feature ownership. Broken features beat broken models as a root cause.\n\n## Official references\n- [Feast docs](https://docs.feast.dev/)\n",
