    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Kafka](https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/), [Flink](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-stable/), and Hadoop-era storage compute patterns.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[Kafka s job in ML/GenAI]\n  N0 --> N1[Flink vs Spark Streaming]\n  N1 --> N2[Is Hadoop still relevant]\n  N2 --> N3[Architect pattern]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Tools Q A Kafka Flink and Hadoop for ML ))\n    Kafka\n    Flink\n    Hadoop\n    Spark\n    Event-Driven Architectur\n    Data Pipelines\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Kafka’s job in ML/GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDurable event log for features, clickstreams, feedback, and async embedding jobs. Consumers rebuild state; producers stay decoupled.\n\n### Q2. Flink vs Spark Streaming?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nFlink: true streaming with low-latency stateful operators. Spark: micro-batch strength + lakehouse SQL. Pick by latency and existing platform skills.\n\n### Q3. Is Hadoop still relevant?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nHDFS/YARN patterns linger; many orgs moved to object storage + Spark/Flink. Understand MapReduce history for interviews; build on lakes today.\n\n### Q4. Architect pattern?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nEvents → stream processor → feature store / lake → training & RAG refresh. Enforce schemas (Avro/Protobuf) and exactly-once where needed.\n\n## Official references\n- [Kafka](https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/) · [Flink](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-stable/)\n",
