    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\n[PyTorch](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/index.html), [TensorFlow](https://www.tensorflow.org/guide), [scikit-learn](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/), [ONNX](https://onnx.ai/).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[PyTorch vs TensorFlow/Keras ]\n  N0 --> N1[Where does scikit-learn fit]\n  N1 --> N2[Why ONNX]\n  N2 --> N3[Jupyter vs production code]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Tools Q A PyTorch TensorFlow Keras Sciki))\n    PyTorch\n    TensorFlow\n    Keras\n    Scikit-Learn\n    ONNX\n    Jupyter\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. PyTorch vs TensorFlow/Keras today?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPyTorch dominates research and many production LLM stacks. TensorFlow/Keras remain strong in some enterprise/mobile TFLite paths. Choose by team skill + serving targets.\n\n### Q2. Where does scikit-learn fit?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nClassical ML baselines, preprocessing, metrics, and tabular pipelines — still essential before jumping to deep models.\n\n### Q3. Why ONNX?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOpen format to export models for portable, optimized runtimes (ONNX Runtime, TensorRT paths). Decouples training framework from serving hardware.\n\n### Q4. Jupyter vs production code?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nNotebooks for exploration; promote to versioned modules + tests for anything that ships. Keep data/model cards with the code.\n\n## Official references\n- [PyTorch](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/index.html) · [TensorFlow](https://www.tensorflow.org/guide) · [scikit-learn](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/) · [ONNX](https://onnx.ai/)\n",
