    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T17:30:28.231Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nDistributed and constrained deployment patterns for ML/GenAI.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAuto-generated from this lesson’s **title, tags, and headings** — refresh anytime with `npm run hub:diagrams`.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Start[GenAI] --> N0[What is federated learning]\n  N0 --> N1[Edge AI tradeoffs]\n  N1 --> N2[When is AutoML useful]\n  N2 --> N3[Architect checklist]\n  N3 --> N4[Key takeaway]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((AI Q A Federated learning Edge AI and Au))\n    Federated Learning\n    Edge AI\n    AutoML\n    Model Lifecycle\n    Edge Computing\n    IoT Integration\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is federated learning?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTrain across devices/silos by aggregating updates (not raw data). Useful for privacy-sensitive settings; hard parts: systems heterogeneity, poisoned updates, and communication cost.\n\n### Q2. Edge AI tradeoffs?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nOn-device inference: lower latency/privacy, limited compute/memory. Techniques: quantization, distillation, small SLMs, NPUs. Sync policies for model updates.\n\n### Q3. When is AutoML useful?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nBaseline search over classical ML pipelines and HPO. For deep/LLM stacks, AutoML helps features/HPO but does not replace architecture/eval design.\n\n### Q4. Architect checklist?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nData residency, update cadence, monitoring on-device failures, and fallback to cloud models when edge confidence is low.\n\n## Key takeaway\nEdge/federated expand the design space — start from constraints (privacy, power, network), not model size.\n",
