    "content": "## Scope\nProduct practice for technical leaders shipping AI capabilities.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Leadership Q&A: Product thinking, ideation, prototyping, accelerators** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Leadership Q&A: Product thinking, ideation))\n    Product Thinking\n    Ideation\n    Prototyping\n    Accelerator Development\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is product thinking for AI Architects?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nStart from user job-to-be-done and measurable outcomes, not model novelty. Define success metrics before choosing RAG vs classical ML.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Ideation hygiene?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nDiverge on problem statements, constrain by data readiness/risk tier, then converge on a thin slice MVP.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What makes a good prototype?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTests the riskiest assumption (retrieval quality, clinician trust, latency) with real-ish data and an eval harness—not a slideware demo.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What is accelerator development?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nReusable packaged capability (templates, eval packs, connectors) that shortens the next team's delivery—with docs and ownership, not a one-off repo dump.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Try this\nOne-pager: problem, user, metric, risk tier, 30-day prototype plan.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nProduct thinking: job-to-be-done, metric, risk tier, thin-slice MVP. CrewAI/LangGraph docs both imply structured Flows/graphs over unbounded autonomy for production. Accelerators are reusable packs with owners/docs — not abandoned demos.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nModel-first roadmaps waste quarters; decision-first roadmaps ship value.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Problem/metric/risk tier one-pager before build\n- Kill criteria for spikes\n- Accelerator checklist: tests, docs, onboarding path\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Demo-driven architecture\n- No path from sandbox to controlled pilot\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
