    "content": "## Scope\nClinical decision support and population analytics practice for AI Architects.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Healthcare Q&A: CDS, population health, predictive care, risk stratification** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Healthcare Q&A: CDS, population health, pr))\n    Clinical Decision Support\n    Population Health\n    Predictive Healthcare\n    Risk Stratification\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is Clinical Decision Support (CDS)?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTools that provide clinicians with knowledge/patient-specific info to enhance decisions. AI CDS should assist—not silently place orders—and expose rationale/citations.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Population health vs individual prediction?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPopulation health manages cohorts (gaps in care, prevention). Individual predictive healthcare estimates a person's risk/outcome. Methods and metrics differ; governance differs too.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is risk stratification?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSegmenting patients by predicted risk to target interventions. Requires calibration, fairness checks across subgroups, and operational playbooks for outreach.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. GenAI role?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSummarize charts, draft outreach, explain cohort criteria—always with human approval for clinical actions.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Self-check\nWrite an intended-use sentence for a readmission model vs a chart-summary copilot.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nCDS assists clinicians with patient-specific info; AI CDS must expose rationale and keep humans in control. Population health manages cohorts; risk stratification segments for intervention with calibration/fairness checks. Align intended use with NIST GenAI risk thinking and HIPAA minimum necessary.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nCDS and risk stratification change care pathways — intended use and oversight are the product.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Intended-use statement signed by clinical owner\n- Calibration + subgroup fairness review\n- Operational playbook for outreach/actions\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Silent autonomous actions\n- Optimizing AUC while calibration is poor for intervention thresholds\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
