    "excerpt": "Managed cloud AI Q&A with official doc anchors for training, hosting, and foundation models. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-cloud-ai-deep",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.145Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[SageMaker](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/), [Bedrock](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/), [Azure ML](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/machine-learning/), [Vertex AI](https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/docs).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **DevOps Q&A: SageMaker, Bedrock, Azure ML, Vertex AI deep dive** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((DevOps Q&A: SageMaker, Bedrock, Azure ML, ))\n    AWS SageMaker\n    AWS Bedrock\n    Azure ML\n    GCP Vertex AI\n    Cloud AI\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. SageMaker vs Bedrock?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSageMaker: build/train/host custom ML/LLM pipelines, feature store, endpoints. Bedrock: managed foundation models/agents via AWS APIs with IAM—less undifferentiateed GPU ops, more model API consumption.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is Azure Machine Learning?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nAzure's workspace-centric platform for jobs, environments, managed endpoints, and Responsible AI tooling integrated with Azure identity/governance.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is Vertex AI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nGCP's unified ML platform: training, prediction, Model Garden, Vector Search, and pipelines—aligned with GCP IAM and data products.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. How do you choose?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nData residency, existing cloud estate, GPU quota, team skills, and exit strategy. Always keep eval harnesses and prompt/model registries portable.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. Serverless AI meaning?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nConsumption-based model APIs / managed endpoints that hide server ops. Still require auth, quotas, logging redaction, and quality monitoring.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [SageMaker](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/) · [Bedrock](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/) · [Azure ML](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/machine-learning/) · [Vertex AI](https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/docs)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nBedrock: managed FMs with inference profiles/batch/provisioned throughput, Agents, Flows, Knowledge Bases (RAG), Prompt management, Guardrails, evaluation, IAM. SageMaker for custom train/host pipelines. Azure ML workspaces/endpoints + Responsible AI tooling. Vertex AI for GCP unified ML + Vector Search/Model Garden. Keep portable eval/registry regardless of cloud.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nManaged AI platforms trade ops burden for lock-in and shared-responsibility gaps.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Residency + network path reviewed with security\n- Exit plan for prompts/weights/indexes\n- Per-env spend quotas\n- Use Bedrock Guardrails/Prompt management when on AWS rather than reinventing basics\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Assuming managed = governed\n- No eval parity between clouds when multi-sourcing models\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
