    "id": 88,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "Data Q&A: Spark, Dask, Airflow, Prefect, and ETL pipelines",
    "tags": [
      "Spark",
      "Dask",
      "Airflow",
      "Prefect",
      "ETL",
      "Data Pipelines"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Scale and orchestration Q&A for large clinical/claims/enterprise datasets. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-spark-dask-orchestrators",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:29.111Z",
    "level": "Advanced",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Apache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/), [Dask](https://docs.dask.org/en/stable/), [Airflow](https://airflow.apache.org/docs/), [Prefect](https://docs.prefect.io/).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **Data Q&A: Spark, Dask, Airflow, Prefect, and ETL pipelines** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Data Q&A: Spark, Dask, Airflow, Prefect, a))\n    Spark\n    Dask\n    Airflow\n    Prefect\n    ETL\n    Data Pipelines\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Spark vs Dask?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSpark: JVM-centric distributed engine, SQL/DataFrame APIs, lakehouse workhorse. Dask: parallelizes native Python/Pandas-like workflows. Choose Spark for large SQL/ETL estates; Dask when Python-native scale-out fits better.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is ETL vs modern ELT?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nETL transforms before load; ELT loads then transforms in the warehouse/lakehouse. GenAI pipelines often ELT raw docs to object storage, then transform to chunks/embeddings.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. Airflow vs Prefect?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nBoth orchestrate DAGs/flows with retries, schedules, and observability. Airflow is widely standardized in enterprises; Prefect emphasizes modern Pythonic flows. Pick one platform per org—avoid dual orchestrators.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Pipeline SLOs for ML/GenAI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nFreshness, completeness, schema contracts, and data quality gates before training/reindex jobs. Failed quality checks should block model promotion.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/) · [Dask](https://docs.dask.org/en/stable/) · [Airflow](https://airflow.apache.org/docs/) · [Prefect](https://docs.prefect.io/)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nSpark for large SQL/ETL/lakehouse; Dask for Python-native scale-out. Airflow (stable docs ~3.x): workflows-as-code, Dags, Task SDK/`@task`, UI backfills; best for clear start/end batch/ML/agentic pipelines—requires coding, not click-only. Prefect is the other common Pythonic orchestrator—standardize on one.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nHealthcare/enterprise volumes need distributed compute and reliable orchestration, not nightly laptops.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- One orchestrator standard per org\n- Freshness + quality gates before train/reindex\n- Idempotent pipeline runs with clear lineage\n- Prefer Airflow 3 Task SDK patterns over legacy 1.x idioms\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Dual Airflow+Prefect estates without need\n- Silent continue-on-error into model training\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
