    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "ML Q&A: Hypothesis testing, experimental design, and A/B testing",
    "tags": [
      "Hypothesis Testing",
      "Experimental Design",
      "A/B Testing",
      "Statistics"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Statistics Q&A for trustworthy model and prompt launches—including guardrail metrics. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-stats-experiments",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.601Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nClassical experimental design applied to ML/GenAI changes.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **ML Q&A: Hypothesis testing, experimental design, and A/B testing** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((ML Q&A: Hypothesis testing, experimental d))\n    Hypothesis Testing\n    Experimental Design\n    A/B Testing\n    Statistics\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is hypothesis testing here?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nFormal check whether an observed uplift is likely noise. Pair statistical significance with **practical** significance (cost, latency, clinical risk).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is experimental design for ML?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPre-register primary metric, sample size, stop rules, and strata. Separate train/val/test. For GenAI, keep offline eval before online exposure.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. How should A/B tests treat safety?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSafety/guardrail metrics are hard gates. A “better” engagement metric that increases harmful outputs is a failed experiment.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Shadow vs A/B?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nShadow runs new model without user-visible change—good for latency/error checks. A/B exposes variants to measure outcome impact.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Self-check\nDraft a one-page experiment brief for a prompt change.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nPre-register primary metric, sample size, stop rules. Offline then online. Guardrail metrics as hard gates. Shadow deployments for operational risk before A/B outcome tests. MLflow/Bedrock evaluation features help operationalize scoring—not replace experiment design.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nShipping prompt/model changes without an experiment design is storytelling, not science.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Experiment brief template required for prod changes\n- Separate novelty metrics from safety metrics\n- Document confounds (UI, seasonality, user mix)\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Peeking and stopping early without a plan\n- Optimizing engagement while harm rate rises\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
