    "title": "ML Q&A: Predictive analytics, classification, forecasting, feature engineering",
    "tags": [
      "Predictive Analytics",
      "Classification",
      "Forecasting",
      "Feature Engineering"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Applied ML Q&A connecting problem types to metrics, features, and leakage-safe pipelines. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-predictive-features",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.595Z",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\nStandard supervised learning practice used in enterprise ML and healthcare analytics.\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **ML Q&A: Predictive analytics, classification, forecasting, feature engineering** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((ML Q&A: Predictive analytics, classificati))\n    Predictive Analytics\n    Classification\n    Forecasting\n    Feature Engineering\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What is predictive analytics?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nUsing historical data to estimate future outcomes or unknown labels—risk scores, demand, readmission, churn—supporting decisions under uncertainty.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. Classification vs forecasting?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nClassification predicts discrete labels (fraud/not). Forecasting predicts future numeric/temporal values (census, volume). Metrics differ (AUC/F1 vs MAPE/wQL).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is feature engineering?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nCreating informative inputs: aggregations, lags, embeddings, clinical codifications. Must preserve **point-in-time correctness** to avoid leakage.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. Common leakage example?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nUsing post-outcome labs or claim finalization fields that would not exist at prediction time.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. GenAI overlap?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLLMs can assist feature ideation/NLP features, but tabular risk models still need classical training, calibration, and monitoring.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Try this\nWrite a feature contract: name, source, freshness SLA, allowed prediction time, owner.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nClassification vs forecasting need different metrics. Feature contracts must include point-in-time availability. GenAI can help create NLP features but tabular risk models still need classical training and monitoring.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nFeature leakage creates beautiful backtests and dangerous production models.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Feature dictionary with owner + SLA + PIT rules\n- Offline/online parity tests\n- Slice metrics for key populations\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Using post-outcome fields in training\n- No monitoring when upstream ETL changes definitions\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
