    "level": "Intermediate",
    "content": "## Scope\n[Langfuse × LangChain/LangGraph](https://langfuse.com/integrations/frameworks/langchain), [Langfuse × LlamaIndex](https://langfuse.com/integrations/frameworks/llamaindex), plus evaluation practice aligned with vendor eval guides such as [OpenAI Evaluation](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/evaluation).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nObservability + evaluation gates before promotion.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Req[Request] --> Span[Trace spans]\n  Span --> LF[Langfuse / LangSmith]\n  Span --> Score[Eval scores]\n  Score --> Gate{Promote?}\n  Gate -->|yes| Prod[Production]\n  Gate -->|no| Fix[Fix prompt / tools]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((LLMOps observe))\n    Traces\n      Prompts\n      Tools\n      Cost\n    Eval\n      Offline\n      Online\n      Agents trajectories\n    Safety\n      Redaction\n      Region / HIPAA\n```\n\n\n### Q1. What does Langfuse capture?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLangfuse positions itself as an AI engineering/observability platform: traces of LLM interactions including inputs, outputs, tool usage, retries, latencies, and costs—so you can debug, analyze, and evaluate. LangChain integration uses **Callbacks**; LlamaIndex commonly via OpenTelemetry instrumentation exporting spans to Langfuse.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is LangTrace in this keyword bank?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nLangTrace refers to LLM/application tracing tooling used similarly to capture spans for prompts, retrieval, and tools. In LangChain's first-party stack, **LangSmith** is the documented tracing/eval platform. Architects should pick one primary trace backend, standardize span names, and enforce redaction policies.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is agent evaluation vs single-turn LLM eval?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nSingle-turn eval scores final text. Agent evaluation scores **trajectories**: tool choice correctness, loop termination, policy adherence, cost/latency, and task success. Offline golden tasks + online sampling with human review is the production pattern.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. What are reflection loops?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nA design pattern where an agent critiques or verifies its own draft (self-reflection / reviewer agent) before acting. Useful for reducing tool mistakes—but must be budgeted (extra tokens) and gated so reflection cannot bypass safety policy.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. What must never land in traces by default?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nRaw PHI/PII, secrets, full clinical notes, or unrestricted EHR payloads. Store redacted prompts, hash identifiers, and link to access-controlled source systems.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q6. Minimum observability checklist for LLMOps?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nPrompt/version IDs, model IDs, retrieval hit metadata, tool spans, token/cost metrics, error rates, eval scores, and user feedback—tied to a model/prompt registry (e.g. MLflow).\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Official references\n- [Langfuse LangChain](https://langfuse.com/integrations/frameworks/langchain) · [Langfuse LlamaIndex](https://langfuse.com/integrations/frameworks/llamaindex) · [OpenAI Evaluation](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/evaluation)\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nLangfuse: CallbackHandler for LangChain/LangGraph; captures LLM/tool/retriever spans, latency, cost; Cloud free tier or self-host; multi-region base URLs including a HIPAA endpoint—pick region for compliance. Attribute traces with user_id/session_id/tags. MLflow GenAI: OpenTelemetry-compatible tracing, LLM-as-judge eval, prompt management/lineage, AI Gateway for access/cost control. Reflection loops must be token-budgeted and policy-gated.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nIf you cannot reconstruct why an agent spent $12 and returned a wrong clinical summary, you cannot improve or audit it.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- One primary trace backend; consistent span naming\n- Capture model id, prompt version, retrieval ids, token/cost\n- Choose Langfuse/LangSmith region that matches data residency (incl. HIPAA endpoint if required)\n- Redact PHI/PII before export\n- Promote prompts only after eval regression is green\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Logging full prompts with PHI to the wrong SaaS region\n- Only scoring final text while agents take bad tool paths\n- No link from production trace → prompt/model registry version\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
