    "excerpt": "In this comprehensive guide, you'll learn everything about network interception and request mocking in Playwright. From quick examples to detailed use cases, we cover best practices and real-world scenarios to elevate your testing skills.",
    "sourceUrl": "",
    "createdAt": "2026-04-03T09:55:17.454Z",
    "level": "beginner",
    "content": "## How to Use Network Interception and Request Mocking Quickly?\n\nTo get started with network interception and request mocking in Playwright, you can set up a simple scenario within minutes. This allows you to intercept HTTP requests and provide custom responses effortlessly.\n\nHere's a quick example:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const context = await browser.newContext();\n  const page = await context.newPage();\n\n  await page.route('**/api/data', route => {\n    route.fulfill({\n      status: 200,\n      contentType: 'application/json',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ success: true })\n    });\n  });\n\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n  await page.click('#fetchData'); // Assume this triggers the API call\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Visual overview\nFrom [Network](https://playwright.dev/docs/network): arm response waits before the action; route for deterministic mocks.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Arm[waitForResponse promise] --> Click[User action]\n  Click --> Req[Browser request]\n  Req --> Route{page.route?}\n  Route -->|yes| Mock[fulfill / continue / abort]\n  Route -->|no| Net[Real network]\n  Mock --> Resp[Response]\n  Net --> Resp\n  Arm --> Resp\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Network))\n    Observe\n      request events\n      waitForResponse\n    Mock\n      page.route\n      context.route\n      fulfill\n    Caveats\n      Service Workers\n      MSW takeover\n    Pattern\n      Arm waiter first\n      Then act\n      Then await\n```\n\n\n## How to Network Interception and Request Mocking in Detail?\n\nNetwork interception in Playwright allows you to monitor and manipulate network requests and responses. This functionality is crucial for testing how your web application behaves under different conditions or when receiving varied data.\n\nTo implement a detailed example, consider a scenario where you want to mock an API response based on certain conditions. The flow involves intercepting the network request, analyzing it, and then deciding whether to fulfill it with a mock response or to let it proceed as is.\n\nThe following code demonstrates how to handle this:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const context = await browser.newContext();\n  const page = await context.newPage();\n\n  await page.route('**/api/resource', async route => {\n    const response = await route.fetch();\n    const data = await response.json();\n\n    if (data.shouldMock) {\n      route.fulfill({\n        status: 200,\n        contentType: 'application/json',\n        body: JSON.stringify({ mocked: true })\n      });\n    } else {\n      route.continue();\n    }\n  });\n\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n  await page.click('#getResource'); // Trigger the API call\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\nIn this example, we check if the data should be mocked or not. If yes, we fulfill the request with a mock response; otherwise, we continue with the original request.\n\n## What is Network Interception and Request Mocking?\n\n**Network interception** is the ability to intercept requests made by the application under test. This feature allows you to inspect and modify requests and responses, making it an essential tool for testing various scenarios without requiring actual server calls.\n\nThe concept of request mocking is closely tied to interception. It involves creating a fabricated response that mimics what a real server would send back. This is particularly useful in cases where the actual server is unavailable or when you want to simulate certain conditions.\n\nPlaywright introduced network interception capabilities as part of its robust automation framework, enhancing testing workflows. It operates at a low level, providing hooks into the network layer of the browser, allowing for fine-grained control over HTTP requests.\n\nIn the ecosystem of web testing tools, this feature distinguishes Playwright from other libraries by enabling developers to create more reliable and faster tests. With network interception, you can test how your application handles various responses, including error states and different data structures.\n\n## When to Use Network Interception and Request Mocking?\n\n- **Testing Error Handling**: Mock error responses to ensure your application gracefully handles failures.\n- **Simulating Slow Networks**: Use request mocking to simulate a slow server response and test loading states.\n- **Bypassing External Dependencies**: Avoid dependence on external APIs during testing by mocking their responses.\n- **Data Consistency**: Ensure consistent data responses for tests by mocking instead of relying on real server states.\n- **Unit Testing Components**: Isolate component tests from server dependencies by mocking necessary data.\n\n### Is Network Interception and Request Mocking different from Stubbing?\n\nYes, network interception is a broader concept. While **stubbing** generally refers to replacing a function with a mock implementation, network interception specifically targets HTTP requests and responses within the browser???s context.\n\n### Does Network Interception and Request Mocking trigger events?\n\nYes, network interception can indeed trigger events. When requests are intercepted, it can lead to changes in application state, which may in turn trigger events within your application.\n\n## What is the Difference Between Intercepting and Mocking? \n\nIntercepting is about capturing and modifying requests before they reach the server, while mocking involves providing a predefined response instead of allowing the request to reach its original endpoint.\n\n| Method          | When to Use               | Key Behavior                  | Speed   |\n|----------------|---------------------------|--------------------------------|---------|\n| Interception    | Need to modify requests    | Allows manipulation before sending| Fast    |\n| Mocking         | Need to simulate responses | Returns predefined data instead | Very Fast |\n| Real Requests    | Test against live APIs     | Real server interaction        | Variable|\n| Combination      | Full control over flow     | Mix of both methods            | Medium  |\n\n## Step by Step Guide with Examples\n\n### Step 1: Setup Playwright Environment\n\nFirst, ensure you have Playwright installed and set up in your project.\n\n```bash\nnpm install playwright\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create a Browser Context\n\nInitialize a new browser context to manage your pages and sessions separately.\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\nconst browser = await chromium.launch();\nconst context = await browser.newContext();\n```\n\n### Step 3: Open a New Page\n\nCreate a new page instance where you can navigate and perform actions.\n\n```js\nconst page = await context.newPage();\n```\n\n### Step 4: Intercept Network Requests\n\nSet up request interception to handle specific requests.\n\n```js\nawait page.route('**/api/data', route => {\n  route.fulfill({\n    status: 200,\n    contentType: 'application/json',\n    body: JSON.stringify({ success: true })\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### Step 5: Trigger the Request\n\nNavigate to the URL that triggers the API request or simulate an action that causes it.\n\n```js\nawait page.goto('https://example.com');\nawait page.click('#fetchData');\n```\n\n### Step 6: Close the Browser\n\nFinally, ensure to close the browser to clean up resources.\n\n```js\nawait browser.close();\n```\n\n## Common Patterns and Variations\n\n### Pattern\n",
